description:
crates/tokenzero-engine/src/workspace.rs:106-124 resolves the recovery cache path with full env awareness, while crates/tokenzero-recovery/src/embedded_store.rs:504-515 has an independent copy that never consults ZEROSTACK_STORE_ROOT or the opt-in:

  let unified = root.join(".zerostack").join("tokenzero").join("recovery-cache.json");
  let legacy = root.join(".tokenzero").join("recovery-cache.json");
  if unified.exists() || root.join(".zerostack").is_dir() { unified } else { legacy }

The doc comment at :502-503 says this is deliberate, to keep the handle env-independent after construction. The consequence is still that the CLI and the embedded handle can select different files for the same root whenever a pin is in play.

The two also disagree on the legacy tiebreak. workspace.rs:113-120 picks unified only when unified.exists() || !legacy.exists(), so a preexisting legacy cache keeps winning; embedded_store.rs uses unified.exists() || .zerostack.is_dir(). TokenZero is the only engine with a history-dependent tiebreak: FSZero (zerostack_store.rs:57-61) and GraphZero (:131-135) switch to unified unconditionally once a store resolves. History-dependent means two processes at different times pick different files for the same repo.

Fix: delete the second copy and pass a captured StoreEnv into the handle at construction, which preserves the env-independence property without duplicating the algorithm. Also replace classify_root_mode (:723-742) with the shared StoreMode.

Refs zerostack-sce, zerostack-pi1.
